## Key Observations

- Reflected XSS occurs when malicious input is returned by the web application and executed in the user's browser.
- Stored XSS persists malicious input on the server and can affect users who later access the affected content.
- Improper input validation and output encoding can allow injected JavaScript to execute in a user's browser.
- Stored XSS can have a greater impact because the malicious input remains available after the initial submission.
- Browser-executed scripts can access information available to the vulnerable web page, depending on security controls.

